The answer is: (a Canadian Indian reserve in British Columbia) were traveling back to their reserve from Merritt, British Columbia, after attending a funeral in July 1952. They were traveling by car and were camping along theFraser Riverin an area near where the Huntley Street Ridge Earthworks are located. The two men were seen by witnesses on the road leading to the reserve, and were never seen again. The RCMP (Royal Canadian Mounted Police) and WCG (Western Canada Group of the Sons of Russia) began searching the area for the two men, but no trace of the men or their vehicle was found. The RCMP finally declared the men missing on October 25, 1952.The case was included in the book The Phantom Trail by Edward Whittemore and R.E.M. Goldberg. Whittemore, a reporter for the Vancouver Sun, investigated the case for several years, and published a story in the Vancouver Sun in 1954. The story was also published in the Vancouver Department of Indian Affairs Agent's Report for 1954. The case was also presented at the 1955 Canadian Congress of Anthropology. Goldberg was a detective who worked on the case for the RCMP. Together, Whittemore and Goldberg established the Phantom Trail Gang, a group which would attempt to solve "cold cases" such as this one. However, despite their efforts, the case remains unsolved.


